  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B030",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B006",
    "B042"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks are a mature and unusually well-defined atomic-physics achievement: magic-wavelength confinement permits an ensemble to realize an exceptionally narrow clock transition without the usual trapping shift, and independent laboratories have reached 10^-18-class performance. This is not simply an incremental accuracy record. It established a robust new way to compare time and frequency, opening experimentally real access to relativistic redshift tests and searches involving fundamental constants. Katori's originating concept and Ye's decisive experimental development remain a compact, complementary, and defensible attribution.",
    "case_against": "The principal reservation remains that this is an enabling measurement architecture, while its most dramatic fundamental discoveries may not yet have occurred. The historical record must also distinguish the lattice-clock invention and its decisive development from the wider optical-frequency-metrology lineage. These qualifications require a narrow citation, but do not negate the completed and independently reproduced physical achievement.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson argues that WMAP delivered a completed empirical transformation, whereas the most consequential discoveries enabled by lattice clocks remain largely prospective.",
        "response": "This is the strongest objection to B030, and it has not been evaded by treating prospective applications as accomplished results. I nevertheless conclude that an experimental invention can be prize-worthy once it has reliably changed the attainable physical regime. The clock architecture has done so repeatedly and quantitatively. WMAP is an important alternative, but its three-person representation of a large mission and the need to delimit its role within the broader construction of precision cosmology remain substantial attribution questions."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors the Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase because they are mature, experimentally tested conceptual transformations whose consequences are already broad.",
        "response": "I agree fully about their depth and enduring influence. But their distinct historical origins are not a minor editorial difficulty: a common citation must avoid turning two separate discoveries into one episode. B030 has a more coherent concept-to-realization line and exceptionally direct reproducibility. I therefore retain B042 as an alternative, rather than replacing the lattice-clock configuration with a combined phase-effects prize."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll maintains that reproducible 10^-18 precision itself opened a qualitatively new experimental regime, and that Katori and Ye form the clearest credit line.",
        "response": "I agree. From atomic physics, independent agreement at this precision is decisive evidence that the central physical principle, not merely one laboratory's technical virtuosity, has matured. The remaining documentary task is to preserve that clean Katori-Ye line without absorbing the broader optical-metrology community into the citation."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical boundary between Katori's magic-wavelength lattice-clock concept, Ye's decisive development, and essential contributions from the broader optical-frequency-metrology community requires documentary confirmation.",
      "Whether the committee should reserve recognition until a specific landmark fundamental-physics discovery is made with optical lattice clocks remains a matter of judgment.",
      "Whether the Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase can be joined under one sufficiently precise historical and physical boundary remains unresolved."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "I have collaborated with Ursula Keller, a credited name on B001; that profile-based connection may require recusal from deliberation on B001. No apparent connection to the preferred B030 laureates is supplied."
  }
}
